CAT = "ukfoodLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("Which Act is the main food safety law in England, Scotland and Wales?",
 "Food Safety Act 1990", ["Food Hygiene Act 2006", "Consumer Rights Act 2015", "Health and Safety at Work Act 1974"],
 "The Food Safety Act 1990 makes it an offence to sell food that is unsafe or not what the customer asked for. Detailed hygiene rules sit in regulations made alongside it; there is no Food Hygiene Act 2006."),
("Who usually inspects restaurants and cafés for food hygiene?",
 "Environmental health officers", ["Trading standards officers", "The police licensing team", "Health and Safety Executive staff"],
 "Food hygiene in catering is enforced by the local authority's environmental health officers (EHOs). Trading standards officers mainly deal with labelling and food standards."),
("An EHO finds a breach that must be put right within a set time. What will they usually serve?",
 "A hygiene improvement notice", ["A hygiene prohibition notice", "A fixed penalty notice", "A closure warning letter"],
 "A hygiene improvement notice lists the breach, what must be done and the deadline. Closing a business or stopping a process is a different step: an emergency prohibition notice for an imminent risk to health, or a prohibition order made by a court."),
("When can an EHO close a food business straight away?",
 "If there is an imminent health risk", ["When its hygiene rating falls below 3", "When a single complaint is received", "When the records are a day or two late"],
 "An imminent risk to health, such as a serious pest infestation or sewage in the kitchen, allows an emergency prohibition. A low rating or a single complaint does not by itself close a business."),
("What must a business show to use the due diligence defence?",
 "It took all reasonable precautions", ["It had never been inspected before", "It sold the food at below cost price", "The customer ate the food too late"],
 "Due diligence means the business took all reasonable precautions and exercised all due diligence to avoid the offence. Good records of checks are the usual evidence."),
("What does food hygiene law say about training food handlers?",
 "They need training suited to their job", ["Only the manager needs any training", "They need a Level 3 certificate to start", "Training is needed only after one year"],
 "Food handlers must be supervised and instructed or trained in food hygiene in a way that suits their work. The law does not name a particular certificate or level."),
("Is holding a food hygiene certificate a legal requirement for food handlers?",
 "No, but suitable training is", ["Yes, before their first shift", "Yes, within the first month", "No, and nor is any training"],
 "The law requires suitable supervision and instruction or training, not a certificate. Many employers use a Level 2 qualification to show the training was done."),
("How long before opening must a new food business register with the local authority?",
 "At least 28 days", ["At least 7 days", "At least 14 days", "At least 3 months"],
 "The Food Standards Agency asks new food businesses to register with their local authority at least 28 days before trading. Registration is free."),
("A food handler spots a food safety hazard during their shift. What is their legal duty?",
 "Report it to their supervisor", ["Wait for the next official inspection", "Note it in the cleaning log", "Mention it to a customer"],
 "Food handlers must report hazards they cannot deal with themselves to the person in charge straight away. Waiting or only writing it down leaves the risk in place."),
("What scale does the food hygiene rating scheme use in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?",
 "0 to 5", ["1 to 10", "A to E", "1 to 3"],
 "Ratings run from 0 (urgent improvement necessary) to 5 (very good). Scotland runs a different scheme with Pass or Improvement Required."),
("In which nation is displaying the food hygiene rating at the premises a legal requirement?",
 "Wales", ["England", "Scotland", "Great Britain as a whole"],
 "Display is compulsory in Wales and in Northern Ireland. In England display is voluntary, though encouraged, and Scotland uses its own inspection scheme."),
("What results does Scotland's food hygiene information scheme give?",
 "Pass or Improvement Required", ["Gold, silver or bronze award", "Green, amber or red rating", "One to five stars on a sign"],
 "Food Standards Scotland runs the Food Hygiene Information Scheme, which shows Pass or Improvement Required. The 0 to 5 scheme is used in the rest of the UK."),
("Which FSA pack helps small catering businesses in England keep a food safety management system?",
 "Safer Food, Better Business", ["CookSafe", "Safe Catering", "The Food Code"],
 "Safer Food, Better Business (SFBB) is the FSA pack for England and Wales. CookSafe is the Scottish equivalent and Safe Catering is used in Northern Ireland."),
("Who is legally responsible for food safety in a catering business?",
 "The food business operator", ["The local authority", "The Food Standards Agency", "Each customer who buys food"],
 "The food business operator, usually the owner or company, holds the legal duty. Staff also have duties, and the local authority enforces the law rather than running the business."),
("A food handler knowingly serves unsafe food. Who could be prosecuted?",
 "The handler and the business", ["Only the owner of the business", "Only the head chef", "Nobody, if no one fell ill"],
 "Offences under the Food Safety Act can be committed by any person, so an employee can be prosecuted as well as the business. No illness needs to happen for unsafe food to be an offence."),
("What is the maximum penalty for a serious Food Safety Act offence tried in the Crown Court?",
 "Unlimited fine and 2 years' prison", ["A £100 fixed penalty notice only", "Up to £5,000 but no prison term", "Up to 10 years in prison and a fine"],
 "On conviction in the Crown Court the most serious offences carry an unlimited fine, up to two years' imprisonment, or both. Lower courts also have wide fining powers."),
("A customer finds a piece of plastic in a burger. Which offence does this point to?",
 "Not of the substance or quality demanded", ["Food sold above the menu price", "Food served cooler than 63°C", "Food sold without a printed receipt"],
 "Selling food that is not of the nature, substance or quality the customer asked for is an offence under the Food Safety Act. A foreign object such as plastic is a typical example."),
("Which body leads on food safety in England, Wales and Northern Ireland?",
 "The Food Standards Agency", ["Food Standards Scotland", "The Department of Health", "The Health and Safety Executive"],
 "The Food Standards Agency (FSA) covers England, Wales and Northern Ireland. Food Standards Scotland does the same job in Scotland."),
("Which body leads on food safety in Scotland?",
 "Food Standards Scotland", ["The Food Standards Agency", "Health Protection Scotland", "The Scottish Food Board"],
 "Food Standards Scotland has been Scotland's food body since 2015. The Food Standards Agency covers the other three nations."),
("When may an environmental health officer enter food premises to inspect?",
 "At any reasonable hour", ["Only with written notice", "Only after a complaint", "Only when the owner is in"],
 "EHOs have a legal power to enter food premises at any reasonable hour, and most visits are unannounced. Notice or a complaint is not required."),
("During an inspection an EHO asks for a sample of cooked chicken. What should you do?",
 "Allow it, as they have that power", ["Ask them to come back later", "Refuse unless the owner is present", "Say they need a court warrant"],
 "Authorised officers can take samples of food to check its safety. Refusing or delaying them could count as obstruction."),
("What happens if a food handler deliberately obstructs an EHO?",
 "They commit an offence", ["Nothing, it is their right", "The rating goes up", "Only the owner can be fined"],
 "Intentionally obstructing an authorised officer is an offence in itself. Staff should cooperate and fetch the manager."),
("What does HACCP stand for?",
 "Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Point", ["Health and Catering Control Plan", "Hygiene Assessment and Cleaning Check Plan", "Hazard Awareness and Chemical Control Point"],
 "HACCP is Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Point. Food law requires businesses to have procedures based on its principles, scaled to the size of the business."),
("In HACCP, what is a critical control point?",
 "A step where control is essential", ["Any step at all in the kitchen", "The step where food is served", "A point the EHO inspects every year"],
 "A critical control point is a step where control is essential to prevent, remove or reduce a hazard to a safe level, such as cooking or chilling."),
("In HACCP, what is a critical limit?",
 "The value separating safe from unsafe", ["The most food a fridge can hold safely", "The number of staff on a shift", "The longest time food can be frozen"],
 "A critical limit is the value that separates acceptable from unacceptable at a critical control point, such as a core temperature of 75°C after cooking."),
("Monitoring shows a critical limit was missed. What should happen?",
 "Take corrective action", ["Note it at month end", "Carry on and recheck tomorrow", "Change the critical limit"],
 "When a critical limit is not met, corrective action is taken straight away, such as cooking for longer or throwing the food away, and it is recorded."),
("Why does a catering business keep food safety records?",
 "To show its controls are working", ["To work out staff pay and holidays", "To track customer numbers each day", "To plan the menu prices"],
 "Records such as temperature checks show the system is working and help the business prove due diligence if something goes wrong."),
("What is the first HACCP principle?",
 "Identify the hazards", ["Keep the records", "Set corrective actions", "Verify the system works"],
 "The first principle is hazard analysis: identify the hazards at each step. Critical control points, limits, monitoring, corrective actions, verification and records follow."),
]
